# Alert 9 – False Application Down Alerts Due to SNMP Monitoring Issue

## Alert

```text
Critical
Resource : BE1 Server
Alert : Multiple Application Down Alerts

Affected Applications :
- HLR Services
- XIUS Services
- CAP Services

Status :
Applications continuously reported as DOWN and UP intermittently.

Monitoring Impact :
Unable to determine the actual health of applications due to continuous false alerts.
```

---

## Investigation

Verified the overall health of the BE1 server.

```bash
top
free -h
uptime
df -h
```

All system resources were within normal operating limits.

Verified that all production application processes were running.

```bash
ps -ef | grep -i hlr
ps -ef | grep -i xius
```

Confirmed that all HLR and XIUS application processes were active and had not restarted unexpectedly.

Reviewed system logs for any application or operating system failures.

```bash
journalctl
dmesg
tail -f /var/log/messages
```

No application crashes, kernel errors, or resource exhaustion were observed.

Since the operating system and applications were healthy, the investigation shifted to the monitoring layer.

Checked the SNMP service.

```bash
systemctl status snmpd
```

Although the SNMP daemon was running, monitoring continued to report intermittent application failures.

Restarted the SNMP service to restore monitoring communication.

```bash
systemctl restart snmpd
```

After the restart, the monitoring team confirmed that polling resumed successfully and the false alerts disappeared.

---

## Root Cause

The production applications and operating system were functioning normally throughout the incident.

The issue was caused by an SNMP communication problem following the previous day's incident, preventing the monitoring platform from collecting accurate application status.

As a result, the monitoring system generated continuous false application down alerts even though all services remained operational.

---

## Resolution

* Verified server CPU, memory, disk, and load.
* Confirmed all HLR and XIUS application processes were running normally.
* Reviewed system logs and found no operating system or application issues.
* Verified the SNMP service status.
* Restarted the SNMP daemon to restore monitoring communication.
* Confirmed with the monitoring team that polling resumed successfully.
* Validated that all false application down alerts were automatically cleared.

